# ORACLE.sha256 — the RDKit oracle data of the 11 MMFF energy fixtures.
#
# mmff-orthogonal-02 ac-008: "energies are bit-for-bit unchanged after the
# deletion, with NO assertion value edited. Loosening a tolerance to make this
# green is a failure, not a pass."
#
# A tolerance is one way to cheat. The other, quieter one is to regenerate the
# reference. These three files per fixture ARE the assertion:
#
#   <name>.sdf              the geometry the oracle was evaluated on (4-decimal
#                           mol-block coords, re-read by RDKit — so the reference
#                           is exact ON THESE COORDS, not on the full-precision
#                           conformer)
#   <name>.energy.json      RDKit MMFFGetMoleculeForceField(mol).CalcEnergy()
#   <name>.breakdown.json   the 7 frozen per-style terms; each file self-validates
#                           against the oracle (sum of terms == mmff94_total_energy)
#
# Editing any byte of any of them moves the target the deletion is measured
# against. That is not allowed to be quiet.
#
# Regenerating these is a legitimate act — but it is an act of a DIFFERENT spec,
# and it requires re-deriving them from RDKit and re-proving self-consistency.
# `test-subset-assertions` is that different spec: it extends the seven original
# MMFF94-only energy rows with RDKit's MMFF94s total so caffeine and e_big cannot
# sit outside the variant oracle. Geometry, MMFF94 totals and breakdowns did not move.
